    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Crop improvement</span> is the deliberate application of genetic and agronomic techniques to develop crop varieties with better yield, quality, disease resistance or other desirable traits.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Objectives of Crop Improvement</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Crop improvement aims to develop crop varieties that produce higher yields, resist pests and diseases, tolerate adverse environmental conditions, mature early, and have better quality (taste, nutrient content, storage life) than existing varieties.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Methods of Crop Improvement</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Selection</td><td>Choosing and propagating plants with the most desirable characteristics from an existing population over successive generations</td></tr><tr><td>Introduction</td><td>Bringing a crop variety from one region or country into a new area where it was not previously grown</td></tr><tr><td>Hybridization</td><td>Deliberately crossing two different varieties or species with desirable traits to produce offspring combining the best features of both parents</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Advantages of Crop Improvement</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Increases crop yield and farmers' income.</li><li>Produces varieties resistant to pests, diseases and drought.</li><li>Improves nutritional quality and taste of produce.</li><li>Shortens maturation period, allowing more harvests per year.</li><li>Expands the range of environments where a crop can be grown (through introduction).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Disadvantages of Crop Improvement</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Can be time-consuming and expensive, especially hybridization programmes.</li><li>May reduce genetic diversity if only a few improved varieties are widely adopted.</li><li>Introduced varieties may become invasive or require specific inputs (fertiliser, pesticides) to perform well.</li><li>Requires skilled personnel and research infrastructure.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of Genetic Improvement</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Genetic improvement underlies long-term food security, as it allows crops to keep pace with growing population needs, changing climate conditions, and emerging pests and diseases, ensuring sustained agricultural productivity.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to distinguish the three methods clearly: selection works within an existing population, introduction brings in a new variety from elsewhere, and hybridization creates something new by crossing two varieties.</div>",
