    "topicCode": "SS3-AGR-03",
    "module": "Animal Production",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Animal health management</span> involves the practices used to prevent, detect and control diseases and parasites affecting livestock, ensuring productivity and welfare.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Importance of Animal Health</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Healthy animals grow faster and produce more meat, milk or eggs.</li><li>Prevents economic losses from illness, death or reduced productivity.</li><li>Reduces the risk of disease transmission to other animals and to humans (zoonotic diseases).</li><li>Supports the overall profitability and sustainability of livestock farming.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Common Diseases of Livestock</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Affected animals</th><th>Symptoms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Newcastle disease</td><td>Poultry</td><td>Twisted neck, respiratory distress, drop in egg production, high mortality</td></tr><tr><td>Foot and mouth disease</td><td>Cattle, sheep, goats, pigs</td><td>Blisters on mouth and feet, fever, lameness, reduced appetite</td></tr><tr><td>Coccidiosis</td><td>Poultry, young ruminants</td><td>Bloody diarrhoea, weakness, poor growth</td></tr><tr><td>Mastitis</td><td>Dairy cattle</td><td>Swollen, painful udder, abnormal milk</td></tr><tr><td>Trypanosomiasis</td><td>Cattle</td><td>Fever, weight loss, anaemia, transmitted by tsetse flies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Common Parasites of Livestock</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Parasite type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>External parasites</td><td>Ticks, mites, lice, fleas</td><td>Skin irritation, disease transmission, reduced weight gain</td></tr><tr><td>Internal parasites</td><td>Roundworms, tapeworms, liver flukes</td><td>Poor growth, anaemia, diarrhoea, reduced productivity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Prevention and Control Measures</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Regular vaccination against common diseases.</li><li>Routine deworming and parasite control programmes.</li><li>Proper sanitation and hygiene in housing and feeding areas.</li><li>Quarantine of newly introduced or sick animals.</li><li>Balanced nutrition to support strong immunity.</li><li>Regular veterinary check-ups and prompt treatment of sick animals.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Routine Management Practices for Healthy Animals</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Providing clean water and balanced feed at all times.</li><li>Maintaining clean, well-ventilated housing.</li><li>Keeping accurate health and vaccination records.</li><li>Controlling stocking density to reduce stress and disease spread.</li><li>Isolating sick animals promptly to prevent disease spread.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to match a disease to its typical symptoms and the group of animals it affects — Newcastle disease (poultry) and foot and mouth disease (cattle/sheep/goats) are especially common exam topics.</div>",
