    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Aquaculture</span> is the farming of aquatic organisms — fish, crustaceans, molluscs and aquatic plants — under controlled or semi-controlled conditions, rather than relying solely on wild capture.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Scope</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Aquaculture involves breeding, rearing and harvesting aquatic species in ponds, tanks, cages or other controlled water bodies, and is an increasingly important source of food and income worldwide.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Aquaculture Systems</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Extensive</td><td>Low stocking density, minimal input (feed/management); relies largely on natural food in the water body</td></tr><tr><td>Semi-intensive</td><td>Moderate stocking density; some supplementary feeding and management inputs are provided</td></tr><tr><td>Intensive</td><td>High stocking density; fully dependent on artificial feeding, aeration and close management</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Common Species Cultured</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Catfish</strong> (Clarias species) — widely farmed in Nigeria for its hardiness and market demand.</li><li><strong>Tilapia</strong> — popular for its fast growth and adaptability.</li><li><strong>Carp</strong> — commonly farmed in various countries for food.</li><li><strong>Shrimp/prawns</strong> — farmed in coastal and brackish water systems.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Management Practices in Aquaculture</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Maintaining good water quality (oxygen level, pH, temperature).</li><li>Providing appropriate, nutritionally balanced feed.</li><li>Regular monitoring for disease and parasites.</li><li>Proper stocking density to avoid overcrowding.</li><li>Routine water exchange or aeration to maintain oxygen levels.</li><li>Timely harvesting to optimise yield and market value.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of Aquaculture in Food Security</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Provides an affordable source of high-quality animal protein.</li><li>Reduces pressure on wild fish stocks, supporting conservation.</li><li>Creates employment and income opportunities, especially in rural areas.</li><li>Can be practised on relatively small plots of land, supporting smallholder livelihoods.</li><li>Helps meet the growing demand for fish as wild catch becomes less reliable.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to distinguish extensive, semi-intensive and intensive systems by their stocking density and level of management input — this is a frequently tested comparison.</div>",
