    "topic": "Apiculture (Bee Keeping)",
    "topicCode": "SS3-AGR-05",
    "module": "Specialised Animal Production",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Apiculture</span>, or bee keeping, is the practice of maintaining honeybee colonies, typically in hives, for the production of honey, wax and other valuable products, as well as for crop pollination.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Importance of Apiculture</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Apiculture is the deliberate rearing and management of honeybees. It is important both as a source of income from bee products and because bees are vital pollinators that improve the yield of many food crops.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Bees in a Colony</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Queen bee</td><td>The single reproductive female in the colony, responsible for laying eggs</td></tr><tr><td>Worker bees</td><td>Sterile females that forage, build comb, care for the brood and defend the hive</td></tr><tr><td>Drones</td><td>Male bees whose primary role is to mate with a queen</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Equipment Used in Bee Keeping</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Beehive</td><td>The structure housing the bee colony</td></tr><tr><td>Smoker</td><td>Produces smoke to calm bees and reduce aggression during hive inspection</td></tr><tr><td>Protective clothing (veil, gloves, suit)</td><td>Protects the beekeeper from stings</td></tr><tr><td>Hive tool</td><td>Used to pry apart hive components and scrape away excess wax or propolis</td></tr><tr><td>Honey extractor</td><td>Spins honeycombs to extract honey without destroying the comb</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Management Practices in Bee Keeping</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Siting hives away from human activity but near good sources of nectar and water.</li><li>Regular hive inspection for colony health, pests and queen performance.</li><li>Providing supplementary feeding during periods of nectar scarcity.</li><li>Protecting hives from pests such as wax moths and predators such as ants.</li><li>Harvesting honey at the appropriate time without harming the colony.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Products of Bee Keeping and Their Economic Uses</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Economic use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Honey</td><td>Food sweetener, traditional medicine, cosmetics</td></tr><tr><td>Beeswax</td><td>Candle making, cosmetics, polishes, crafts</td></tr><tr><td>Propolis</td><td>Traditional medicine and health products</td></tr><tr><td>Royal jelly</td><td>Health and nutritional supplements</td></tr><tr><td>Pollination services</td><td>Improves yield of many fruit and vegetable crops grown nearby</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the three types of bees in a colony and their distinct roles — queen (reproduction), workers (all colony tasks), drones (mating) — as this is commonly tested.</div>",
