    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Agricultural finance</span> refers to the funds and credit facilities required to establish, operate and expand farming and agribusiness activities.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Importance</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Agricultural finance covers all sources of capital used to meet the costs of farm inputs, labour, equipment, land and other operational needs. Adequate finance is essential for farmers to invest in improved seeds, fertilisers, equipment and expansion.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sources of Agricultural Finance</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Formal sources</td><td>Commercial banks, microfinance banks, the Bank of Agriculture, cooperative societies, government agricultural loan schemes</td></tr><tr><td>Informal sources</td><td>Personal savings, family and friends, local thrift and credit associations (e.g. esusu/ajo), moneylenders</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Problems of Agricultural Finance</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>High interest rates charged by formal lenders.</li><li>Stringent collateral requirements that many smallholder farmers cannot meet.</li><li>Bureaucratic delays in loan processing and disbursement.</li><li>Limited access to formal financial institutions in rural areas.</li><li>Risk of crop failure or price fluctuation making repayment difficult.</li><li>Poor record-keeping by farmers, limiting their creditworthiness.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Role of Government and Banks in Agricultural Credit</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Government provides subsidised loans, grants and input support schemes to encourage farming.</li><li>Specialised institutions such as the Bank of Agriculture offer tailored agricultural credit products.</li><li>Government-backed guarantee schemes reduce the risk for commercial banks lending to farmers.</li><li>Banks provide savings, credit and advisory services to support farm investment and growth.</li><li>Central bank policies (e.g. targeted lending programmes) can direct funds specifically toward agriculture.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to classify a given source of finance (e.g. esusu, Bank of Agriculture, cooperative society) correctly as formal or informal.</div>",
