    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Agricultural insurance</span> is a risk management tool that protects farmers against financial losses arising from unpredictable events affecting crops, livestock, equipment or produce.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Importance</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Agricultural insurance is a contract in which a farmer pays a premium to an insurer in exchange for compensation if specified losses occur, such as crop failure from drought, disease outbreak in livestock, or damage to farm equipment.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Agricultural Insurance</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Crop insurance</td><td>Losses from drought, flood, pest attack, disease or other crop failures</td></tr><tr><td>Livestock insurance</td><td>Death or loss of livestock due to disease, accident or theft</td></tr><tr><td>Farm equipment insurance</td><td>Damage to or loss of machinery, tools and farm structures</td></tr><tr><td>Produce insurance</td><td>Loss of harvested produce in storage or transit, e.g. from fire or spoilage</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Benefits of Agricultural Insurance</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Provides financial protection against unpredictable losses.</li><li>Encourages farmers to adopt improved, sometimes riskier, technologies and practices.</li><li>Improves farmers' access to credit, as insured farms are seen as lower-risk by lenders.</li><li>Stabilises farm income across good and bad seasons.</li><li>Supports the overall stability of the agricultural sector and food supply.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Limitations of Agricultural Insurance</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Premiums can be unaffordable for many smallholder farmers.</li><li>Limited awareness and understanding of insurance products among farmers.</li><li>Difficulty in accurately assessing and verifying claims in rural areas.</li><li>Limited availability of agricultural insurance products in some regions.</li><li>Potential for moral hazard, where insured farmers may take less care to prevent losses.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Role of Government and Insurance Companies</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Government may subsidise premiums to make insurance more affordable for farmers.</li><li>Government agencies (e.g. the Nigerian Agricultural Insurance Corporation) provide specialised agricultural insurance products.</li><li>Insurance companies develop tailored products addressing specific agricultural risks.</li><li>Public awareness campaigns help farmers understand the value and process of agricultural insurance.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to match a scenario (e.g. 'a farmer's cattle died of disease') to the correct type of agricultural insurance (livestock insurance).</div>",
